    "task_fidelity": "All three comparisons survive in one natural native multiple-choice unit. A/D address the direction of finite inclusion; B/E discriminate equality from the proper even-integer subset; F checks both failed inclusions for the fraction/positive pair, with C diagnosing confusion between fraction notation and membership in Q minus Z. This is mathematical recognition of claims and reasons, not independent proof production.",
    "redundancy": "No duplicate correct alternatives or cross-question answer reuse exist. A/D repeat premises but reverse the inclusion direction; B/E distinguish a false universal evenness claim from strict containment; C/F use different membership and containment claims. Pairwise contrast permits legitimate elimination but still requires the targeted set judgments.",
    "answer_leakage": "The view contains no key, original exercise number, correctness marker or required number of selections. Supplied witnesses are the material to judge, not an external correctness cue. Similar declarative wording appears in both true and false alternatives. The larger statement in F is mathematically needed to refute two directions; length alone does not determine the exact set.",
    "independent_correct": ["A", "E", "F"],
    "option_checks": [
      {
        "option_id": "A",
        "truth": "correct",
        "evidence": "The three elements -1, 0 and 3 of the first A all occur in B. -4 is in B and not A, so A is a proper subset of B."
      },
      {
        "option_id": "B",
        "truth": "incorrect",
        "evidence": "Even numbers have form 2k with integral k, but 1 is an integer and cannot equal 2k for integral k. A and B are not equal."
      },
      {
        "option_id": "C",
        "truth": "incorrect",
        "evidence": "1=2/2 and 1 is positive, but 1 is integral. Under the permitted p0195#10 definition A=Q minus Z, so 1 is not in A and not in the intersection."
      },
      {
        "option_id": "D",
        "truth": "incorrect",
        "evidence": "The premises support A contained in B, not B contained in A. -4 in B but not A directly refutes the stated direction."
      },
      {
        "option_id": "E",
        "truth": "correct",
        "evidence": "For integral k, 2k is integral, so every even number lies in A. 1 is an integer but not even, making B a proper subset of A."
      },
      {
        "option_id": "F",
        "truth": "correct",
        "evidence": "-1/2 is rational nonintegral and negative, witnessing A not contained in B. 1 is positive integral, witnessing B not contained in A. Neither direction holds, without assuming an ambient irrational domain."
      }
    ],
